\title[Pole Order, Locality and Newton Recursions]{Pole Order, Locality and Newton Recursions\\ for Products of Normalized $q$-Series}

\author{Aristotle}

\date{11 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\CC((q))$ be the field of formal Laurent series over $\CC$. Call a series
\emph{normalized} if it has the shape $f = q^{-1} + a_0 + a_1 q + a_2q^2 + \cdots$:
a simple pole at $q = 0$ with residue $1$ and no deeper pole. Series of this shape include
the McKay--Thompson series of monstrous moonshine, one for each of the $194$ conjugacy
classes of the Monster group. Multiplying $q$ into such a series yields a unit
$u_f = qf$ of $\CC[[q]]$, so a product of $m$ normalized series factors as $q^{-m}$ times
a unit. Indexing coefficients by \emph{level} --- $c_k$ is the coefficient of $q^{k-m}$ ---
we prove: a closed multi-Cauchy master formula for $c_k$, uniform in $k$ and free of case
distinctions; a locality theorem showing $c_k$ depends on at most $k$ of the $m$ factors
and only on their coefficients in degrees $< k$, improving to $k/2$ factors when all
constant terms vanish; a Newton recursion $(k+1)c_{k+1} = \sum_{j \le k}c_j p_{k-j}$ driven
by the logarithmic derivatives of the unit parts, with base case $c_0 = 1$; a rigidity
theorem stating that the logarithmic power sums $p_0,\dots,p_{K-1}$ determine
$c_0,\dots,c_K$ regardless of the number of factors; and an integrality transfer principle.
Specializing to one-term tails recovers the elementary symmetric functions, the classical
Newton identities, and their invertibility in characteristic zero, all from the arithmetic
of a single pole. Two complementary algorithms are given and cross-validated numerically,
including on a $194$-factor product of moonshine shape.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Formal Laurent series, pole order, McKay--Thompson series, monstrous moonshine,
Newton's identities, elementary symmetric functions, logarithmic derivative, locality}

