\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
A recurring shape in modular forms, vertex operator algebra character theory and moonshine
is the \emph{normalized $q$-series}
\[
  f(q) = \frac{1}{q} + a_0 + a_1 q + a_2 q^2 + \cdots .
\]
The canonical example is the Hauptmodul of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\ZZ)$,
\[
  J(q) = j(q) - 744 = \frac1q + 196884\,q + 21493760\,q^2 + \cdots,
\]
whose coefficients are dimensions of graded pieces of the moonshine module. Monstrous
moonshine attaches to each conjugacy class $g$ of the Monster $\mathbb{M}$ a
McKay--Thompson series
\[
  T_g(q) = \frac1q + 0 + c_g(1)q + c_g(2)q^2 + \cdots,
\]
the graded trace of $g$ on the moonshine module. There are $194$ such classes, hence $194$
such series, each with a simple pole of residue $1$ and vanishing constant term.

Products of such series arise in denominator-formula manipulations, in the study of
replicable functions, and whenever one wishes to treat the whole family
$\{T_g\}_g$ simultaneously. The question addressed here is elementary to state:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Question.} Given $m$ normalized series $f_1,\dots,f_m$, what are the Laurent
coefficients of $\prod_i f_i$, and how much of the data of the factors does each one see?
\end{quote}

A naive expansion looks hopeless: one coefficient of a product of $194$ infinite series
appears to require summing over an unmanageable set of choices. The results below show
the answer is highly structured. The organizing observation is a triviality with
substantial consequences: \emph{a simple pole of residue $1$ factors off as a unit.}

\subsection{Prior state and contribution}
For a product of $m$ normalized series it was already known that the pole order is exactly
$m$ and that $q^m\prod_i f_i$ is a unit of $\CC[[q]]$; the resulting shift identity
identifies the Laurent coefficient of $\prod f_i$ in degree $k-m$ with the power-series
coefficient in degree $k$ of $\prod(qf_i)$. On the coefficient side, however, only two
instances were available: the subleading identity at level $1$,
$\coef{1-m}\prod_i f_i = \sum_i a_0(f_i)$, and a level-$2$ identity quadratic in $a_0$ and
linear in $a_1$. The general level $k$ was open, except in the degenerate \emph{linear}
case $f_i = q^{-1}+a_i$, where the answer is the elementary symmetric function $e_k(a)$.

This paper closes the level-$k$ problem in three independent ways --- a closed formula, a
locality decomposition, and a Newton recursion --- proves the recursion invertible, and
derives from that inversion both a rigidity theorem for moonshine-type products and a proof
of the classical Newton identities and their invertibility that never touches
symmetric-function combinatorics.

\section{Setting and basic structure}

Let $\CC[[q]]$ be the ring of formal power series and $\CC((q))$ the field of formal
Laurent series, whose elements have coefficients indexed by $\ZZ$ with only finitely many
nonzero in negative degrees. For $F\in\CC((q))$ and $n \in \ZZ$ write $\coef{n}F$ for the
coefficient of $q^n$.

\begin{definition}[Normalized series]
A series $f\in\CC((q))$ is \textbf{normalized} if $\coef{-1}f = 1$ and $\coef{n}f = 0$ for
all $n < -1$. Equivalently $f = q^{-1}+a_0+a_1q+\cdots$ with $a_n := \coef{n}f$. We call
$(a_0,a_1,a_2,\dots)$ the \textbf{tail} of $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Unit part]
The \textbf{unit part} of a normalized $f$ is
$u_f := qf = 1 + a_0q + a_1q^2 + a_2q^3+\cdots \in \CC[[q]]$, so that
$\coef{n+1}u_f = \coef{n}f$ for $n \ge -1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Unit factorization]\label{lem:unit}
For normalized $f$, the series $u_f$ lies in $\CC[[q]]$ and has constant term $1$; hence
$u_f$ is a unit of $\CC[[q]]$ and $f = q^{-1}u_f$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Multiplication by $q$ shifts degrees up by one, cancelling the order-one pole and leaving
no negative degrees; the new constant term is $\coef{-1}f = 1$. A power series is a unit
exactly when its constant term is invertible.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Pole order and shift]\label{prop:shift}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ be normalized with $|S| = m$. Then
$\prod_{i\in S}f_i = q^{-m}\prod_{i\in S}u_{f_i}$, the second factor being a unit of
$\CC[[q]]$. Consequently the product has a pole of order exactly $m$ and, for all $k\ge0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:shift}
  \coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i \;=\; \coef{k}\prod_{i\in S}u_{f_i}.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:unit} and multiplicativity: $\prod_i(q^{-1}u_i) =
q^{-m}\prod_i u_i$, and a finite product of units is a unit, so the constant term of
$\prod_i u_i$ is $1 \neq 0$, giving pole order exactly $m$. Identity \eqref{eq:shift} is
the reindexing induced by the shift $q^{-m}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Level-$k$ coefficient]
For a family $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ of normalized series with $|S| = m$, the \textbf{level-$k$
coefficient} is $c_k := \coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i$, for $k \in \ZZ_{\ge0}$.
\end{definition}

Indexing by level rather than absolute degree is the essential bookkeeping device: it makes
products of different sizes directly comparable, and by \eqref{eq:shift} identifies $c_k$
with an ordinary power-series coefficient. Note $c_0 = \coef{0}\prod_i u_i = 1$.

\begin{definition}[Exponent vectors]
For a finite set $S$ and $k \ge 0$ let
$\mathcal{A}(S,k) := \{\nu : S\to\ZZ_{\ge0} \mid \sum_{i\in S}\nu_i = k\}$
be the set of \textbf{weak compositions} of $k$ indexed by $S$, and
$\supp\nu := \{i\in S : \nu_i \neq 0\}$. One has
$|\mathcal{A}(S,k)| = \binom{m+k-1}{k}$ with $m = |S|$.
\end{definition}

\section{The master formula}

\begin{theorem}[Level-$k$ master formula]\label{thm:master}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ be normalized, $|S| = m$. Then for every $k \ge 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:master}
  \coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i \;=\; \sum_{\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)}\ \prod_{i\in S}\
  \coef{\nu_i - 1}f_i .
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:shift} it suffices to compute $\coef{k}\prod_i u_{f_i}$. The coefficient of
$q^k$ in a finite product of power series is the multi-Cauchy sum
\[
  \coef{k}\prod_{i\in S}u_{f_i} = \sum_{\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)}\prod_{i\in S}\coef{\nu_i}u_{f_i},
\]
obtained by iterating the binary Cauchy product. Since $\coef{\nu_i}u_{f_i} =
\coef{\nu_i-1}f_i$ for every $\nu_i \ge 0$, substitution gives \eqref{eq:master}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Uniformity]
Formula \eqref{eq:master} needs no case distinction, and this is not accidental. A factor
with $\nu_i = 0$ is queried at degree $-1$, where normalization gives $\coef{-1}f_i = 1$:
the pole coefficient is precisely the neutral element of the product. Any restatement in
terms of tail coefficients alone forces a split into cases and loses this uniformity.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Formula \eqref{eq:master} is the arithmetic shadow of $\prod_i f_i = q^{-m}\cdot(\text{unit})$:
the $q^{-m}$ accounts for the reindexing $k \mapsto k-m$, the unit for the composition sum.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Pole coefficient]\label{cor:pole}
$c_0 = 1$: the coefficient of $q^{-m}$ in $\prod_{i\in S}f_i$ equals $1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\mathcal{A}(S,0) = \{0\}$, and the single term is $\prod_{i\in S}\coef{-1}f_i = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-factor convolution form]\label{cor:two}
For normalized $f,g$,
$\coef{k-2}(fg) = \sum_{j=0}^{k}\coef{j-1}f\cdot\coef{k-j-1}g$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Specialize \eqref{eq:master} to $|S| = 2$, where $\mathcal{A}(S,k)$ is parametrized by
$j = \nu_1 \in\{0,\dots,k\}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Locality, truncation, half locality, integrality}

Theorem~\ref{thm:master} sums over $\binom{m+k-1}{k}$ terms, which is large when $m$ is.
Almost all of that complexity is vacuous.

\begin{lemma}[Exponent bound]\label{lem:expbound}
If $\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)$ then $\nu_i \le k$ for every $i \in S$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\nu_i \le \sum_{j\in S}\nu_j = k$, all terms being nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Support bound]\label{lem:supp}
If $\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)$ then $|\supp\nu| \le k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Summing $\nu$ over its support gives $k$, since omitted terms vanish. Each summand over the
support is $\ge 1$, so $|\supp\nu| = \sum_{i\in\supp\nu}1 \le \sum_{i\in\supp\nu}\nu_i = k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Unexcited factors are neutral]\label{lem:neutral}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ be normalized and $\nu$ an exponent vector with $\supp\nu\subseteq S$. Then
$\prod_{i\in S}\coef{\nu_i-1}f_i = \prod_{i\in\supp\nu}\coef{\nu_i-1}f_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $i\notin\supp\nu$ we have $\nu_i = 0$ and the factor is $\coef{-1}f_i = 1$; delete these.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Locality decomposition]\label{thm:local}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ be normalized, $|S| = m$. For every $k \ge 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:local}
  \coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i \;=\;
  \sum_{\substack{T\subseteq S\\|T|\le k}}\ \
  \sum_{\substack{\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)\\ \supp\nu = T}}\ \
  \prod_{i\in T}\coef{\nu_i-1}f_i .
\end{equation}
In particular $c_k$ is a universal polynomial, independent of $m$, in the tail coefficients
of at most $k$ of the factors at a time.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition $\mathcal{A}(S,k)$ into the fibres of $\nu\mapsto\supp\nu$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:supp} every fibre lies over a subset $T\subseteq S$ with $|T|\le k$, so the
fibration is well defined onto $\{T\subseteq S : |T|\le k\}$. Applying
Lemma~\ref{lem:neutral} inside each fibre replaces the product over $S$ by that over $T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The inner sum in \eqref{eq:local} runs over strict compositions of $k$ into $|T|$ positive
parts, of which there are $\binom{k-1}{|T|-1}$. The total number of nonvacuous terms is
$\sum_{t\le\min(k,m)}\binom{m}{t}\binom{k-1}{t-1}$: polynomial in $m$ of degree $k$ rather
than exponential, and the interactions involve at most $k$-element subsets.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Truncation locality]\label{thm:trunc}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ and $(g_i)_{i\in S}$ be families of normalized series with $|S| = m$,
and let $k\ge0$. If $\coef{j}f_i = \coef{j}g_i$ for all $i\in S$ and all $0\le j<k$, then
$\coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i = \coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}g_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Compare master formulas term by term. Fix $\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)$ and $i \in S$. If
$\nu_i = 0$, both factors equal $\coef{-1}f_i = 1 = \coef{-1}g_i$. If $\nu_i = r+1\ge1$
then $r < k$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:expbound}, and the hypothesis gives
$\coef{r}f_i = \coef{r}g_i$. Corresponding products, hence the sums, agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No exponent equals one]\label{thm:noones}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ be normalized with $\coef{0}f_i = 0$ for all $i\in S$. Then
\[
  \coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i \;=\;
  \sum_{\substack{\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)\\ \nu_i \neq 1\ \forall i\in S}}\
  \prod_{i\in S}\coef{\nu_i-1}f_i .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\nu_i = 1$ for some $i$, the corresponding factor is $\coef{0}f_i = 0$ and the whole
product vanishes; discard those terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Half locality]\label{thm:half}
If $\nu\in\mathcal{A}(S,k)$ satisfies $\nu_i\neq1$ for all $i \in S$, then
$2|\supp\nu| \le k$. Consequently, under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:noones}, the
level-$k$ coefficient depends on at most $\lfloor k/2\rfloor$ of the factors at a time.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every $i \in \supp\nu$ has $\nu_i \neq 0$ and $\nu_i \neq 1$, hence $\nu_i \ge 2$. Summing
over the support, $2|\supp\nu| \le \sum_{i\in\supp\nu}\nu_i = k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monster half locality]
For a product of the $194$ McKay--Thompson series, the coefficient of $q^{k-194}$ is a
universal polynomial in the tails of at most $\lfloor k/2\rfloor$ of the $194$ classes.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Integrality transfer]\label{thm:integrality}
Let $R\subseteq\CC$ be a subring and $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ normalized with $\coef{n}f_i\in R$
for all $i \in S$ and $n \in \ZZ$. Then $\coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}f_i \in R$ for all $k\ge0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:master} the coefficient is a finite sum of finite products of elements
of $R$, and $R$ is closed under sums and products.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If every McKay--Thompson-shaped factor has integer coefficients then every Laurent
coefficient of the $194$-fold product is an integer. (Here $\coef{-1}f_i = 1 \in R$ and
$\coef{n}f_i = 0 \in R$ for $n<-1$, so the hypothesis reduces to integrality of the tails.)
\end{corollary}

\section{The logarithmic derivative and the Newton recursion}

\begin{definition}[Logarithmic derivative]
For $u\in\CC[[q]]$ with $u(0)\neq0$, set $\Lder(u) := u'u^{-1}\in\CC[[q]]$, where $'$ is the
formal derivative and $u^{-1}$ the power-series inverse.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The logarithmic derivative linearizes products]\label{thm:logderiv}
Let $(u_i)_{i\in S}$ be power series with $u_i(0)\neq0$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logderiv}
  \Bigl(\prod_{i\in S}u_i\Bigr)' \;=\; \Bigl(\prod_{i\in S}u_i\Bigr)\cdot\sum_{i\in S}\Lder(u_i).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $|S|$. For $S = \varnothing$ both sides vanish. For the step, write
$P = \prod_{i\in S}u_i$ and adjoin $a\notin S$. Leibniz gives $(u_aP)' = u_a'P + u_aP'$, and
by the inductive hypothesis $P' = P\sum_{i\in S}\Lder(u_i)$, so
\[
  (u_aP)' = u_aP\Bigl(u_a'u_a^{-1} + \sum_{i\in S}\Lder(u_i)\Bigr),
\]
using $u_au_a^{-1} = 1$, legitimate because $u_a(0)\neq0$. This is \eqref{eq:logderiv} for
$S\cup\{a\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Logarithmic power sums]
For a family $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ of normalized series with unit parts $u_i = qf_i$, put
$p_r := \coef{r}\sum_{i\in S}\Lder(u_i) = \sum_{i\in S}\coef{r}(u_i'/u_i)$ for $r \ge 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Newton recursion]\label{thm:newton}
With $c_k$ the level-$k$ coefficients of $\prod_{i\in S}f_i$ and $p_r$ the logarithmic
power sums,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:newton}
  (k+1)\,c_{k+1} \;=\; \sum_{j=0}^{k}c_j\,p_{k-j}\qquad(k\ge0), \qquad c_0 = 1.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $P = \prod_{i\in S}u_i$, so $c_j = \coef{j}P$ by \eqref{eq:shift}. Extract the
coefficient of $q^k$ from \eqref{eq:logderiv}. On the left,
$\coef{k}(P') = (k+1)\coef{k+1}P = (k+1)c_{k+1}$, since differentiation sends
$q^{k+1}\mapsto(k+1)q^k$. On the right the Cauchy product gives
$\sum_{j=0}^{k}\coef{j}P\cdot\coef{k-j}\sum_i\Lder(u_i) = \sum_{j=0}^{k}c_jp_{k-j}$.
The base case is Corollary~\ref{cor:pole}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Constant term of the logarithmic derivative]\label{prop:ct}
For normalized $f$ with unit part $u = qf$, $\coef{0}\Lder(u) = \coef{0}f = a_0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$u$ has constant term $1$, hence so does $u^{-1}$, and
$\coef{0}(u'u^{-1}) = \coef{0}(u')\cdot\coef{0}(u^{-1}) = \coef{1}u = \coef{0}f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Level one, two ways]\label{cor:levelone}
$c_1 = \sum_{i\in S}\coef{0}f_i$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
From \eqref{eq:newton} with $k=0$: $c_1 = c_0p_0 = p_0$, and $p_0 = \sum_i\coef{0}f_i$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:ct}. Alternatively, the master formula at $k = 1$ sums over the $m$
indicator vectors $\nu = \delta_i$, whose terms are $\coef{0}f_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Level two]
$2c_2 = \bigl(\sum_i a_0(f_i)\bigr)^2 - \sum_i a_0(f_i)^2 + 2\sum_i a_1(f_i)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The master formula at $k=2$ splits the compositions of $2$ into vectors $2\delta_i$
(contributing $a_1(f_i)$) and $\delta_i+\delta_j$ with $i\neq j$ (contributing
$a_0(f_i)a_0(f_j)$); summing the latter over unordered pairs gives
$\tfrac12[(\sum_i a_0)^2 - \sum_i a_0^2]$.
\end{proof}

\section{Inverting the recursion: rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
Let $(f_i)_{i\in S}$ and $(g_i)_{i\in T}$ be families of normalized series with $|S| = m$
and $|T| = n$ \emph{not assumed equal}. Write $p^f_r,p^g_r$ for their logarithmic power sums
and $c^f_j,c^g_j$ for their level-$j$ coefficients. Fix $K \ge 0$ and suppose $p^f_r = p^g_r$
for all $r < K$. Then $c^f_j = c^g_j$ for all $j \le K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $j$. For $j = 0$ both sides are $1$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:pole}. Let
$j = k+1 \le K$ and assume the claim below $j$. In $\sum_{i=0}^{k}c_ip_{k-i}$ each index
satisfies $i \le k < j \le K$, so $c^f_i = c^g_i$; and $k-i \le k < K$, so
$p^f_{k-i} = p^g_{k-i}$. The right-hand sides of \eqref{eq:newton} agree, giving
$(k+1)c^f_{k+1} = (k+1)c^g_{k+1}$. As $\charac\CC = 0$ we have $k+1\neq0$, and cancellation
yields $c^f_{k+1} = c^g_{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The number of factors has disappeared from the conclusion: two products may have poles of
different orders $m \neq n$, yet the theorem compares them level by level, i.e.\ in degrees
$j - m$ versus $j - n$. In this indexing the head of a product of normalized series is a
function of the sequence $(p_r)_r$ alone. Characteristic zero is used exactly once, in the
cancellation of $k+1$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Sharpness]
Knowing $p_0,\dots,p_{K-1}$ controls exactly the levels up to $K$. The linear families
$\{2\}$ and $\{1,1\}$ share $p_1 = 2$, so their levels $0$ and $1$ agree, while
$p_2 = 4 \neq 2$ and their level-$2$ coefficients differ ($0$ versus $1$).
\end{remark}

\section{The linear case: classical symmetric function theory}

Let $a : S\to\CC$ be a finite family and take the \textbf{linear} normalized series
$\ell_i := q^{-1}+a_i$, with tail $(a_i,0,0,\dots)$ and unit part $u_i = 1+a_iq$.

\begin{proposition}[Coefficients are elementary symmetric functions]\label{prop:esymm}
For every $k\ge0$,
$\coef{k-m}\prod_{i\in S}\ell_i = e_k(a) := \sum_{|T| = k}\prod_{i\in T}a_i$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
In the master formula $\coef{\nu_i-1}\ell_i$ equals $1$ if $\nu_i = 0$, $a_i$ if $\nu_i=1$,
and $0$ if $\nu_i \ge 2$. So the surviving $\nu \in \mathcal{A}(S,k)$ are exactly the
indicator vectors of $k$-element subsets $T$, each contributing $\prod_{i\in T}a_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Logarithmic derivative of a linear unit]\label{prop:linlog}
For $a\in\CC$ and $r\ge0$, $\coef{r}\Lder(1+aq) = (-1)^r a^{r+1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$(1+aq)' = a$ and $(1+aq)^{-1} = \sum_{r\ge0}(-a)^rq^r$, as one checks by multiplying out
(the product telescopes to $1$). Hence $\Lder(1+aq) = a\sum_r(-a)^rq^r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:linps}
In the linear case $p_r = (-1)^r\pi_{r+1}(a)$, where $\pi_s(a) := \sum_{i\in S}a_i^{\,s}$ is
the classical $s$-th power sum.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Newton's identities]\label{thm:newtonesymm}
For any finite family $a : S\to\CC$ and any $k \ge 0$,
\[
  (k+1)\,e_{k+1}(a) \;=\; \sum_{j=0}^{k}(-1)^{k-j}\,e_j(a)\,\pi_{k-j+1}(a).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:newton} to $(\ell_i)_{i\in S}$, substituting $c_j = e_j(a)$ from
Proposition~\ref{prop:esymm} and $p_{k-j} = (-1)^{k-j}\pi_{k-j+1}(a)$ from
Corollary~\ref{cor:linps}.
\end{proof}

The classical low cases follow at once: $k = 0$ gives $e_1 = \pi_1$, and $k = 1$ gives
$2e_2 = \pi_1^2-\pi_2$.

\begin{theorem}[Power sums determine elementary symmetric functions]\label{thm:invnewton}
Let $a : S\to\CC$ and $b : T\to\CC$ be finite families, with $|S|$ and $|T|$ not assumed
equal, and $K \ge 0$. If $\pi_{r+1}(a) = \pi_{r+1}(b)$ for all $r < K$, then $e_j(a) = e_j(b)$
for all $j \le K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:linps} the hypothesis says the logarithmic power sums of the two
linear families agree below degree $K$ (the sign $(-1)^r$ is common). Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}
gives equality of level-$j$ coefficients for $j \le K$, which by
Proposition~\ref{prop:esymm} are the elementary symmetric functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:invnewton} is the classical invertibility of Newton's identities in
characteristic zero, obtained here without symmetric-function combinatorics: the whole
argument runs through poles of Laurent series and one division by $k+1$. Together with
Theorem~\ref{thm:newtonesymm} it shows the pole-order machinery does not merely resemble
symmetric-function theory --- it contains it as the degenerate case of one-term tails.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

Throughout, $m$ is the number of factors and $K$ the highest level required; arithmetic
operations in the coefficient ring are the unit of cost.

\begin{algorithmbox}[Master formula evaluation]
\end{algorithmbox}
\begin{lstlisting}
INPUT : tails A[1..m][0..K-1] of normalized series; level k <= K
OUTPUT: c_k

total <- 0
for each nu in WeakCompositions(m, k):
    term <- 1
    for i in 1..m:
        if nu[i] = 0 then term <- term * 1        # pole coefficient
        else               term <- term * A[i][nu[i]-1]
        if term = 0 then break                    # early exit
    total <- total + term
return total
\end{lstlisting}

Cost: $\binom{m+k-1}{k}$ compositions, i.e.\ $O(m^k/k!)$ for fixed $k$. With the locality
decomposition of Theorem~\ref{thm:local} one instead enumerates subsets $T$ of size
$t\le\min(k,m)$ together with strict compositions of $k$ into $t$ positive parts, giving
$\sum_{t\le\min(k,m)}\binom{m}{t}\binom{k-1}{t-1}$ terms; under the vanishing-constant-term
hypothesis only $t\le\lfloor k/2\rfloor$ occurs. This is the method of choice for structural
reasoning and small $k$, since each term is individually meaningful.

\begin{algorithmbox}[Newton recursion via logarithmic derivatives]
\end{algorithmbox}
\begin{lstlisting}
INPUT : tails A[1..m][0..K-1]; precision K
OUTPUT: c_0..c_K

# 1. unit parts
for i in 1..m: U[i] <- [1, A[i][0], A[i][1], ..., A[i][K-1]]

# 2. logarithmic derivatives and their sum
P[0..K-1] <- 0
for i in 1..m:
    D <- FormalDerivative(U[i])
    V <- PowerSeriesInverse(U[i])       # O(K^2)
    P <- P + Truncate(D * V, K)

# 3. recursion
c[0] <- 1
for k in 0..K-1:
    s <- 0
    for j in 0..k: s <- s + c[j]*P[k-j]
    c[k+1] <- s / (k+1)
return c
\end{lstlisting}

Cost: $O(mK^2)$ for step~2 and $O(K^2)$ for step~3. The recursion itself is independent of
$m$: once the power sums are aggregated the factors are forgotten. For moonshine-scale
computations ($m = 194$) with modest $K$ this is by far the cheaper route, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} explains why no information is lost.

Because the two algorithms are logically independent --- one a Cauchy expansion, the other a
calculus identity --- agreement of their outputs is a meaningful correctness test.

\section{Worked instances}

\noindent\textbf{Two factors, level three.} With $f = q^{-1}+2+3q$ and $g = q^{-1}+5+7q$,
$m = 2$ and level $k = 3$ is the coefficient of $q^1$. Corollary~\ref{cor:two} gives
$\coef{1}(fg) = 1\cdot0 + 2\cdot7 + 3\cdot5 + 0\cdot1 = 29$, confirmed by direct expansion.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Half locality.} For $f = q^{-1}+3q+4q^2$ and $g = q^{-1}+7q+9q^2$ (both with
vanishing constant term) the four compositions of $3$ contribute $1\cdot9$, $0\cdot7$,
$3\cdot0$ and $4\cdot1$, so the level-$3$ coefficient is $13$. The mixed terms die exactly as
Theorem~\ref{thm:noones} predicts, consistent with $2|\supp\nu| \le 3$.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Logarithmic derivative.} By Proposition~\ref{prop:linlog},
$\coef{3}\Lder(1+2q) = (-1)^3 2^4 = -16$.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Rigidity, sharp form.} Linear families $a = (3)$ and $b = (1,2)$ satisfy
$\pi_1(a) = 3 = \pi_1(b)$, so $p_0$ agrees and Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} with $K = 1$ forces
levels $0$ and $1$ to agree ($e_0 = 1$, $e_1 = 3$), though the pole orders are $1$ and $2$.
At the next degree $\pi_2(a) = 9 \neq 5 = \pi_2(b)$ and the level-$2$ coefficients differ
($0$ versus $2$).

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{A moonshine-scale product.} For $m = 194$ factors with integral,
vanishing-constant-term tails, the pole order is exactly $194$; the level-$0$ coefficient is
$1$; the level-$1$ coefficient is $0$; the level-$3$ coefficient equals the sum of the $194$
coefficients $a_2$, since half locality forbids two simultaneous excitations at weight $3$;
and all coefficients are integers by Theorem~\ref{thm:integrality}. Numerical experiments
confirm agreement of both algorithms with direct multiplication of truncated unit parts.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What makes the theory work}
Everything rests on one structural fact: a simple pole with residue $1$ is a unit in
disguise. From it flow the exact pole order $m$; the level indexing that makes products of
different sizes comparable; the uniformity of the master formula, because $\coef{-1}f = 1$
is the multiplicative neutral element; the base case $c_0 = 1$ anchoring the Newton
recursion and hence rigidity; and the invertibility of the unit parts, without which the
logarithmic derivative is undefined. The normalization is the mechanism, not a convenience.

\subsection{Locality as a physical statement}
Theorem~\ref{thm:local} reads as a locality principle: at level $k$ only $k$ sites (factors)
are excited, and total excitation is conserved. Theorem~\ref{thm:half} says a selection rule
(vanishing constant term) forbids single quanta, halving the number of participating sites.
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} says the aggregate observables $p_0,p_1,\dots$ determine the state.
The dictionary is predictive: if $a_0 = \cdots = a_{d-1} = 0$ for every factor, every
surviving exponent is $0$ or $\ge d+1$, whence $(d+1)|\supp\nu| \le k$ by the argument of
Theorem~\ref{thm:half}.

\subsection{Relation to symmetric function theory}
The linear case is exactly classical symmetric function theory, with $c_k \leftrightarrow e_k$
and $p_r \leftrightarrow (-1)^r\pi_{r+1}$. The general case is a deformation in which each
``variable'' $a_i$ is replaced by a full tail: $e_k$ becomes the level-$k$ coefficient, the
$k$-subset sum becomes the composition sum, and both Newton's identities and their
invertibility persist verbatim. What is lost is the vanishing of $e_k$ for $k>m$ --- with
genuine tails all levels are generically nonzero --- and that is precisely the room in which
moonshine-type series live.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{Level-$k$ closed forms in the tails.} Express $c_k$ as an explicit universal
polynomial in the tail coefficients --- the analogue of the Newton--Girard determinant --- to
obtain a canonical basis-free form. The cases $k \le 2$ are the level-one and level-two
corollaries above.
\item \textbf{Higher-order poles.} Replace $q^{-1}$ by $q^{-d}$ with normalization
$\coef{-d}f = 1$. The unit factorization survives, but the neutral-element coincidence that
makes the master formula uniform does not; identifying the correct replacement is the first
question.
\item \textbf{Infinite products.} Moonshine denominator formulas involve infinite products.
Under a suitable $q$-adic convergence hypothesis, does the locality decomposition give a
well-defined level-$k$ coefficient for infinitely many factors? Locality suggests yes: at
level $k$ only $k$ factors ever interact.
\item \textbf{Sharper selection rules.} Vanishing of $a_0,\dots,a_{d-1}$ improves locality to
$k/(d+1)$. Are there arithmetic hypotheses on moonshine coefficients (congruences,
replicability) yielding further restrictions on surviving exponent vectors?
\item \textbf{Integrality beyond subrings.} Congruence information --- divisibility of
level-$k$ coefficients modulo small primes --- is not captured by the subring argument and
would require exploiting cancellation in the composition sum.
\item \textbf{Rigidity as a moduli statement.} Rigidity says the map from families of
normalized series to sequences of logarithmic power sums has fibres on which the head of the
product is constant. Characterizing image and fibres would turn rigidity into a classification.
\item \textbf{Effective inversion.} Recovering the power sums from the coefficients is the
triangular inverse of the recursion, also invertible in characteristic zero. Making both
directions numerically stable, and analysing coefficient growth for moonshine-scale $m$, is
an applied question of independent interest.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Products of normalized $q$-series --- the shape ubiquitous in moonshine --- have completely
structured Laurent coefficients. The pole order is exactly the number of factors; the leading
coefficient is always $1$; each level-$k$ coefficient is a uniform composition sum, sees at
most $k$ of the factors (at most $k/2$ under the McKay--Thompson normalization) and only
their first $k$ tail coefficients; the coefficients satisfy a Newton recursion driven by the
logarithmic derivatives of the unit parts; and that recursion is invertible, so the power
sums form a complete invariant of the head of the product. Specializing to one-term tails
recovers the elementary symmetric functions, Newton's identities, and their classical
invertibility --- all from the arithmetic of a single pole.

